WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.210
Speaker 1: Today, we're going to be speaking
with Democratic Congressman Raja Krishnamoorthi,

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:08.850
who represents the 8th District of Illinois,
that's the western Chicago suburbs.

00:00:08.850 --> 00:00:13.420
He's chairman of the House Oversight Committee
on Economic and Consumer Policy, also a member

00:00:13.420 --> 00:00:16.510
of the very important House Intelligence Committee.

00:00:16.510 --> 00:00:18.320
Congressman, it's great to have you on today.

00:00:18.320 --> 00:00:21.529
Speaker 4: Hey, thank you so much, David.

00:00:21.529 --> 00:00:22.529
Same here.

00:00:22.529 --> 00:00:23.529
Speaker 1: So, OK.

00:00:23.529 --> 00:00:26.680
I mean, to start, we spoke today about for
the first time in his presidency and the real

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:31.869
clear politics average Joe Biden's approval
has dipped just slightly below 40 percent.

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:32.869
It's not great.

00:00:32.869 --> 00:00:37.370
It's lower than at this point, even where
Donald Trump was at this point in his presidency,

00:00:37.370 --> 00:00:38.770
and that was not good.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:44.750
I attribute this to sort of a lull in what
we might call major accomplishments to point

00:00:44.750 --> 00:00:45.790
to.

00:00:45.790 --> 00:00:51.120
What do you think is driving these numbers
and what would you like to see the Biden administration

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:52.120
do?

00:00:52.120 --> 00:01:01.880
Speaker 4: Well, I think that COVID inflation
and probably the lack of progress on certain

00:01:01.880 --> 00:01:09.830
initiatives in Congress contribute to this,
but obviously, as you know, elections are

00:01:09.830 --> 00:01:11.600
about choices.

00:01:11.600 --> 00:01:17.690
And I think that we're going to have some
stark choices in the fall.

00:01:17.690 --> 00:01:21.300
And but before then, we absolutely have to
do better in Congress.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:28.380
But also hopefully we can see COVID decline
and inflation come under some control.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:34.441
Speaker 1: Do you as an individual who's up
for reelection every, every two years, do

00:01:34.441 --> 00:01:39.610
you worry when the president's approval is
low that it will affect your reelection?

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:49.260
Speaker 4: Well, I think that the president's
standing definitely affects how different

00:01:49.260 --> 00:01:56.070
elected officials and Democrats kind of fare
because of the environment that the president's

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:58.570
standing will influence.

00:01:58.570 --> 00:02:05.710
But I think at the end of the day, you know,
voters also try to balance local concerns

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:11.600
and how they perceive their local elected
officials performance based on their own experiences,

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:17.370
for instance, to assess, you know, whether
to support that person.

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:20.720
And so I think the same will be true in this
election.

00:02:20.720 --> 00:02:25.830
All that being said, we need to make sure
that the president is successful when he's

00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:29.120
successful, everyone successful.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.819
But to do that, we have to make sure that
we deliver for the American people and address

00:02:32.819 --> 00:02:35.610
their everyday concerns.

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:37.890
Right now,
Speaker 1: when it comes to something like

00:02:37.890 --> 00:02:45.600
the Russia Ukraine situation, I mean, I think
that bad faith actors will criticize the president

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:52.990
and Democrats no matter what is done, either
as being weak or too strong or not being friendly

00:02:52.990 --> 00:02:57.000
enough or, you know, whatever the case may
be, bad faith actors will have their criticisms.

00:02:57.000 --> 00:03:04.239
But what would you like to see the the the
role of the Biden administration in the United

00:03:04.239 --> 00:03:07.430
States on the basis of what happens there
in the next few months?

00:03:07.430 --> 00:03:10.680
Speaker 4: Well, actually, I think that the
Biden administration has done a tremendous

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:17.300
job of pulling together our allies onto the
same page with regard to aggression on Ukrainian

00:03:17.300 --> 00:03:18.840
new Ukrainian borders.

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.470
And I think that.

00:03:22.470 --> 00:03:30.550
What we need to see at this point is more
multilateral coordination with regard to what

00:03:30.550 --> 00:03:34.700
the costs Russia would face were to invade
Ukraine.

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:42.450
And that means agreeing on a very strong sanctions
regime, agreeing on what we would do to support

00:03:42.450 --> 00:03:45.489
Ukraine if it is invaded.

00:03:45.489 --> 00:03:51.010
And of course, you know, what do we do to
give Russia an offering with regard to the

00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:52.709
current crisis?

00:03:52.709 --> 00:03:59.269
If we can do those three things energetically
and in a coordinated fashion, I think that

00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:01.870
hopefully we'll get to a better place.

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:04.800
But right now, obviously is a very dangerous
moment.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:09.170
Speaker 1: What what does your gut tell you
and you know you're on the House Intelligence

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:13.010
Committee, but so so your gut and what intelligence
you've been presented.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:16.729
There may be some sort of a gray area there,
but what does your gut tell you about the

00:04:16.729 --> 00:04:19.199
real objective here of Putin?

00:04:19.199 --> 00:04:24.529
Is this to suss out how people will react
without a real plan to invade?

00:04:24.529 --> 00:04:32.960
Speaker 4: It's very interesting, and I had
the privilege of attending a classified briefing

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:39.409
with CIA director Bill Burns, who's a tremendous
public servant, by the way.

00:04:39.409 --> 00:04:45.259
We had this to our conversation on Monday
and without getting into classified details.

00:04:45.259 --> 00:04:53.409
I think that what I can say for sure is that
Putin has a very emotional connection to Ukraine

00:04:53.409 --> 00:05:00.569
in the sense that it's almost like a maniacal
obsession with making sure Ukraine does not

00:05:00.569 --> 00:05:05.379
further kind of drift into the Western orbit.

00:05:05.379 --> 00:05:13.629
Interestingly, all of his actions since 2014
the invasion of Crimea, the the presence of

00:05:13.629 --> 00:05:23.479
Russians in the Donbass have further pushed
Ukrainians into the embrace of Western countries.

00:05:23.479 --> 00:05:30.159
The second observation I have is that I do
think that Putin, if he is just operating

00:05:30.159 --> 00:05:37.509
as a rational actor and setting aside his
maniacal kind of personal obsession with Ukraine,

00:05:37.509 --> 00:05:44.789
knows that all things being equal, he doesn't
want to have a situation where he invades

00:05:44.789 --> 00:05:51.600
and then has another Afghanistan on his hands,
just as the Soviet Union did 30 years ago.

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:58.909
And I think that because of that, I do think
that there will be a search for diplomatic

00:05:58.909 --> 00:06:05.349
options that allow him to save face, even
at the same time that he, you know, kind of

00:06:05.349 --> 00:06:07.289
rattles the sabers.

00:06:07.289 --> 00:06:14.979
My hope is that his head prevails over his
heart and that we can kind of convince or

00:06:14.979 --> 00:06:19.119
persuade him that the costs would just be
prohibitive for him.

00:06:19.119 --> 00:06:24.999
And you know, at the end of the day, he might
want to have on his tombstone that he was

00:06:24.999 --> 00:06:28.220
the one who joined Ukraine to Russia.

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:31.990
But the alternative could, just as be, could
be just as plausible, which is he's the one

00:06:31.990 --> 00:06:33.960
that lost Ukraine.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.979
And so I think that's kind of the balance
right now.

00:06:36.979 --> 00:06:43.379
Speaker 1: Is there a discussion among your
colleagues about the possibility that in the

00:06:43.379 --> 00:06:49.789
the sort of using the Ukraine Russia situation
as cover that China may see an opportunity

00:06:49.789 --> 00:06:51.849
to escalate aggression with Taiwan?

00:06:51.849 --> 00:06:53.759
Is that a topic of consideration?

00:06:53.759 --> 00:06:56.410
Speaker 4: It is.

00:06:56.410 --> 00:07:02.949
I think there's two reasons why potentially
she may not.

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:10.490
The Chinese Premier Xi Jinping may not move
on Taiwan right now, which is a I don't think

00:07:10.490 --> 00:07:16.319
that China is prepared militarily to invade
Taiwan.

00:07:16.319 --> 00:07:23.879
And I my perception is that they don't think
that they have enough of a military advantage

00:07:23.879 --> 00:07:26.800
over the United States in that region.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:27.839
That's my perception.

00:07:27.839 --> 00:07:29.339
That's how I feel.

00:07:29.339 --> 00:07:34.559
And then the second is he himself is trying
to consolidate political power this year in

00:07:34.559 --> 00:07:40.330
China with the I believe it's the Chinese
People's Congress that's happening in September

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:41.559
or October.

00:07:41.559 --> 00:07:51.249
And so he's also focused on making sure that
he can bring all the elites along to basically

00:07:51.249 --> 00:07:59.050
rubber stamp his being in office for longer
than perhaps any other premier in many years.

00:07:59.050 --> 00:08:06.569
Speaker 1: A couple of other things, and there's
really so much to talk about in terms of inflation.

00:08:06.569 --> 00:08:11.949
Similarly to gas prices, we're certainly a
president can do a little bit on some of these

00:08:11.949 --> 00:08:18.429
issues, but really, these are big structural,
systemic things that are often global in nature.

00:08:18.429 --> 00:08:20.669
What can be done about inflation?

00:08:20.669 --> 00:08:22.089
What should be done about inflation?

00:08:22.089 --> 00:08:26.809
Speaker 4: Well, I think it's a real issue,
and you don't have to go further than your

00:08:26.809 --> 00:08:31.629
grocery store or your gas pump to see that
it's a real issue, and anybody who doesn't

00:08:31.629 --> 00:08:34.030
think so is denying reality.

00:08:34.030 --> 00:08:39.600
I think there are three things that we as
lawmakers and the Biden administration can

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:40.600
do to tackle this.

00:08:40.600 --> 00:08:49.340
First is we have to fight COVID and we have
to make sure that we see a decline in cases

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:50.890
here, but also abroad.

00:08:50.890 --> 00:08:55.910
So, David, in that regard, I'm going to put
in a big plug for my legislation and my moves

00:08:55.910 --> 00:09:01.190
to really get the world vaccinated at this
point, not just the United States, because

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:06.370
if we see variant after variant come from
abroad, including the Delta variant, which

00:09:06.370 --> 00:09:11.520
killed three of my distant relatives in India
and then the AMA crime variant and others

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:17.070
come to our shores, it's going to wreak more
havoc with our economy and lead to more inflation.

00:09:17.070 --> 00:09:21.140
So that's the first thing we have to control
COVID here and abroad.

00:09:21.140 --> 00:09:27.360
The second thing we have to do is, you know,
those infrastructure investments, which we

00:09:27.360 --> 00:09:35.810
approved in the fall have to be directed at
short term logistical bottlenecks that kind

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:38.340
of continue to hamper the supply chain.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:43.660
So just as an example, we should direct as
much of that money as possible in the short

00:09:43.660 --> 00:09:52.870
term to modernizing our ports, to making sure
that our rail facilities are modern and capable

00:09:52.870 --> 00:10:00.200
of handling the surge in goods as happened
now having pop up inland ports and so forth.

00:10:00.200 --> 00:10:07.510
The third thing that we have to do is we have
to we have to call a spade a spade and note

00:10:07.510 --> 00:10:11.080
that some companies are engaging in predatory
pricing practices.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:17.040
And on my committee, the House Oversight Subcommittee
on Economic and Consumer Policy, which is

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:24.090
a mouthful we launched an investigation into
the meat processing industry, which where

00:10:24.090 --> 00:10:31.330
four firms control more than 60 percent of
meat processing and have increased prices

00:10:31.330 --> 00:10:37.510
by double digits year after year and are seeing
net profits go through the roof.

00:10:37.510 --> 00:10:40.580
And the same thing is happening in other industries.

00:10:40.580 --> 00:10:44.460
And so if we do do those three things, I think
it starts to help.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:50.000
But really, what we need to do is make sure
that we can take the disruptions out of the

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:51.000
supply chain.

00:10:51.000 --> 00:10:53.420
That will be the biggest help at this point.

00:10:53.420 --> 00:10:57.780
Speaker 1: And to pick a couple of those things,
I mean, on vaccinating the world.

00:10:57.780 --> 00:11:02.200
When you look at a world map right now, we're
basically talking about Africa, where there's

00:11:02.200 --> 00:11:03.600
the serious lack of vaccines.

00:11:03.600 --> 00:11:08.480
I mean, even if you know you look at South
America, there's, you know, in Eastern Europe,

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:10.820
there's still fewer vaccine doses.

00:11:10.820 --> 00:11:15.710
But but availability is increasing rapidly,
really everywhere but Africa, as far as I

00:11:15.710 --> 00:11:19.520
understand looking at the map, so is that
the area that should be the focus?

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:26.110
Speaker 4: I think that has to be among the
top places that we direct our efforts.

00:11:26.110 --> 00:11:33.270
You know, in Africa, it's still, I think under
20 percent of the continent of 1.2 billion

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:35.540
people are vaccinated.

00:11:35.540 --> 00:11:42.110
And I bet you dollars to donuts that the next
variant, if it emerges, is going to be from

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:44.550
a place like Africa.

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:51.270
And what we fear is that the lethality of
the next variant could far exceed armed crime

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:54.730
and still have its contagiousness.

00:11:54.730 --> 00:11:56.601
And that's what we have to be really worried
about.

00:11:56.601 --> 00:12:02.210
And I I'm concerned that we have a real blind
spot here, David, to this issue.

00:12:02.210 --> 00:12:08.370
And that's why I and Jeff Merkley and others
are pushing very hard for the White House

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:16.600
to direct more funds, much more funds in this
direction because it's a real, not really

00:12:16.600 --> 00:12:22.520
a modest investment compared to the price
that we've paid to repair our economy and

00:12:22.520 --> 00:12:24.510
our health care system in other ways.

00:12:24.510 --> 00:12:27.540
Speaker 1: So what about domestically at this
point?

00:12:27.540 --> 00:12:33.330
I mean, I have a feeling that due to political
reasons sometime between now and the State

00:12:33.330 --> 00:12:38.030
of the Union on March 1st, the White House
will likely do some sort of a pseudo mission

00:12:38.030 --> 00:12:41.410
accomplished type transition to the endemic
phase statement.

00:12:41.410 --> 00:12:46.080
And whether that's medically prudent is a
question that remains to be seen.

00:12:46.080 --> 00:12:47.290
But you know, in Illinois, J.B.

00:12:47.290 --> 00:12:51.030
Pritzker has said it's going to be time to
start winding down mass requirements.

00:12:51.030 --> 00:12:54.640
We're seeing it happen in California, New
York, some places in schools.

00:12:54.640 --> 00:12:57.060
Does that make sense to you right now based
on the data?

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:59.920
What are the milestones we should be looking
at otherwise?

00:12:59.920 --> 00:13:02.950
Speaker 4: It's an important question.

00:13:02.950 --> 00:13:05.250
I think that.

00:13:05.250 --> 00:13:10.040
To some degree, it makes sense to wind down
some of these mandates because at the end

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:16.710
of the day, we are seeing a rapid decline
in COVID cases.

00:13:16.710 --> 00:13:22.710
And I think there's a lot of public pressure
as well to start to wind down some of those

00:13:22.710 --> 00:13:23.710
mandates.

00:13:23.710 --> 00:13:28.310
However, we have to be prudent about that
because especially in schools and other places

00:13:28.310 --> 00:13:34.600
where a lot of people are yet to be vaccinated
or ineligible to be vaccinated, we just have

00:13:34.600 --> 00:13:36.380
to be cautious.

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:42.830
And so I think that striking that balance
is extremely important right now.

00:13:42.830 --> 00:13:46.290
But I think that at the end of the day, we
have to stick to the science, David.

00:13:46.290 --> 00:13:50.770
We can't just wish this pandemic away.

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:55.110
We're going to have to be very cautious because,
look, we all want to keep the schools open.

00:13:55.110 --> 00:13:57.090
We all want to keep businesses open.

00:13:57.090 --> 00:14:01.980
We want the economy to stay open and we cannot
ever go into lockdown.

00:14:01.980 --> 00:14:07.650
So to prevent that, we have to take these
very basic precautions, such as mask wearing,

00:14:07.650 --> 00:14:09.540
masks, distancing and so forth.

00:14:09.540 --> 00:14:16.770
Speaker 1: OK, last thing I want to talk about
Nancy Pelosi in December said no to a congressional

00:14:16.770 --> 00:14:19.710
stock trading ban in January.

00:14:19.710 --> 00:14:23.680
She said she's open to it and now she's actually
supporting it.

00:14:23.680 --> 00:14:28.840
Can you talk a little bit about your thoughts
on the issue, but also the specifics of how

00:14:28.840 --> 00:14:35.220
such a ban might work in the sense that a
ban on making trades of individual stocks

00:14:35.220 --> 00:14:38.700
while in office is a very sort of narrow thing?

00:14:38.700 --> 00:14:43.020
And how this applies to significant others
or family members, how this applies to mutual

00:14:43.020 --> 00:14:44.020
funds and other things.

00:14:44.020 --> 00:14:45.340
I mean, what would you like to see?

00:14:45.340 --> 00:14:46.340
What makes sense?

00:14:46.340 --> 00:14:49.810
Speaker 4: I think what I like to see is my
bill become law.

00:14:49.810 --> 00:14:50.860
It's the bank.

00:14:50.860 --> 00:14:56.080
Conflicted Trading Act is the main bill that's
being discussed right now.

00:14:56.080 --> 00:15:02.820
It has bicameral, bipartisan support, which
is unusual for anything these days in Congress.

00:15:02.820 --> 00:15:07.190
And it's, I think, the subject of negotiations
at this point.

00:15:07.190 --> 00:15:13.400
Basically, what it would do is this David,
which is what we know is that the stock act

00:15:13.400 --> 00:15:15.030
does not work.

00:15:15.030 --> 00:15:21.980
That is the act which basically requires disclosures
of trading of individual stocks and securities,

00:15:21.980 --> 00:15:23.790
but doesn't prohibit it.

00:15:23.790 --> 00:15:29.320
What we found is that, according to a recent
report, fifty four members of Congress violated

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:35.550
just those disclosure requirements, but hundreds
of others followed those requirements.

00:15:35.550 --> 00:15:40.710
But it created a tremendous appearance of
insider trading.

00:15:40.710 --> 00:15:45.910
You know, basically, people assume that when
members of Congress pull out their Ameritrade

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:50.880
app and start trading stocks, that they're
doing so based on nonpublic information they

00:15:50.880 --> 00:15:55.920
gain from classified briefings and hearings
and insight into legislation.

00:15:55.920 --> 00:16:01.700
So the consensus in the grassroots among Democrats
and Republicans is the current system does

00:16:01.700 --> 00:16:03.210
not work.

00:16:03.210 --> 00:16:11.550
What my bill does is the following it basically
bans or prevents or prohibit members from

00:16:11.550 --> 00:16:18.440
trading individual stocks and securities,
allows them to remain invested and to trade

00:16:18.440 --> 00:16:22.470
mutual funds and widely diversified funds.

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:29.370
It also, if people wish to trade, they can
do so through a blind trust.

00:16:29.370 --> 00:16:33.060
If they wish to keep their stocks, they can,
but they can't trade them.

00:16:33.060 --> 00:16:37.910
They have to hold them for the the time that
they're in office.

00:16:37.910 --> 00:16:43.590
And at the end of the day, what we're trying
to do, David, is we're trying to restore a

00:16:43.590 --> 00:16:47.770
little bit of trust back in Congress here.

00:16:47.770 --> 00:16:54.000
And when every story comes out saying that
some member traded funds, you know, bought

00:16:54.000 --> 00:17:00.160
Pfizer's stock because they got word that
Pfizer just developed a vaccine or sold Pfizer

00:17:00.160 --> 00:17:06.829
stock when they just got word that the pandemic
is actually receding.

00:17:06.829 --> 00:17:10.409
That just creates the appearance that they're
trading on insider information, and it erodes

00:17:10.409 --> 00:17:51.270
trust further.

00:17:51.270 --> 00:18:31.549
S

